=== SURVEY ===
Web source components/ui/Toggle.tsx is a controlled WAI-ARIA switch primitive. Props: label? (the
optional visible text AND, via aria-labelledby, the accessible name), checked (required), onChange
(checked => void), size? ('sm' | 'md', default 'md'), className?, plus the spread of the remaining div
attributes. Render: an inline-flex wrapper (gap-2, cursor-pointer) holding a role="switch" <button>
(aria-checked, the track tinted bg-cyan-500 when on / bg-gray-300 when off, two size variants for the
track h-5 w-9 / h-6 w-11 and the thumb) followed — when label is truthy — by a <span> (text-sm
font-medium text-secondary). Behaviour: the button toggles onChange(!checked); clicking the label text
also toggles via the wrapper's onClick; useId generates the label-association id.
Data sources (P1/S8): useId — ID generation, NOT a data fetch. Shared components used: none. Charts /
maps: none (Recharts->Swift Charts, Leaflet->MapKit mappings N/A here). i18n keys extracted from source:
none (the surface is anonymous — the label is caller-supplied).
Existing native: no Toggle/ToggleSwitch/ToggleModel/ToggleInput/ToggleProjection/ToggleResolved/
ToggleMeta/ToggleSize/ToggleTelemetry/ToggleStrings/ToggleAccessibility symbols anywhere (grep clean =>
no collision). The unrelated ToggleCommandTile (feature-views) is a different surface (a command tile).
Canonical surface pattern studied + mirrored: shared-surfaces/Slider (0226) — the closest precedent, a
controlled no-fetch primitive with the Adapter/Projection/Model/Views/root/Previews/strings/Tests/
AdapterTests split — and the RangePicker (compare toggle) for the native switch idiom.

=== REASONING ===
- The web component is a controlled primitive with NO data dependency: it reads its `checked` prop and
  `useId`, performs no fetch. There is therefore no loading / empty / error / stale / offline axis. The
  template's generic state list does not apply; synthesising network chrome would INVENT state the
  source does not have. This is the predecessor-accepted disposition for synchronous primitives (Slider
  0226, RangeReadout 0087, Distance 0085, AnimatedNumber 0075). The genuine render branches reproduced
  are exactly the web's: the on/off state (track tint), the optional trailing label, and the size
  variant ('sm' / 'md'). Documented in-source in Toggle.Adapter "Parity note — states".
- Native idiom: the parity of a web role="switch" button + hand-drawn track/thumb is SwiftUI's native
  switch (SwiftUI.Toggle in the .switch style), the same idiom the sibling RangePicker compare toggle
  uses. The public view is named `ToggleSwitch` (NOT `Toggle`) so it does not shadow `SwiftUI.Toggle`,
  the control it composes — the same disambiguation Slider made (SliderField vs SwiftUI.Slider). The
  switch is .labelsHidden() + a trailing Text so the on-screen order matches the web (switch, then
  label); tapping the label flips the switch (web "clicking the label text also toggles"). The two web
  Tailwind track sizes map to ControlSize (.small / .regular) at the view boundary so the control stays
  HIG-idiomatic rather than a bespoke shape. Accent tint = Color.TS.accent (web cyan-500); label type/
  colour = Font.TS.label / Color.TS.textSecondary; gap = TSSpacing.sm (8pt == web gap-2). All from P1/S9.
- i18n: the source renders no translatable copy of its own. The ONE native-owned string is a fallback
  accessible NAME for the unlabeled switch — the web sets aria-labelledby to undefined when no label is
  given, leaving the control unnamed; the native surface always voices a name. Resolved through the
  injected P1/S10 facade (NSLocalizedString) with an English fallback; key in the per-surface "Toggle"
  table. No English literal is hardcoded in Swift.
- Accessibility: the native switch carries the switch trait, the spoken on/off value, and the system
  "double tap to toggle" hint; this surface adds the accessible NAME (label or fallback) +
  accessibilityIdentifier. VoiceOver, Dynamic Type, and reduce-motion are respected (native switch +
  system fonts; no custom animation added).
- Telemetry: ToggleModel emits a single P1/S11 `view.opened` event with the slug "Toggle" on first
  appear (idempotent across re-appears), injectable for tests.

